To solve the rectangular packing problem using MiniZinc, we need to define the positions of each item within a larger rectangle and ensure that no items overlap. The objective is to minimize the area of the larger rectangle.

Here's the MiniZinc model for the problem:

```minizinc
% Input data
int: num_items = 4;
array[1..num_items] of int: widths = [3, 4, 2, 1];
array[1..num_items] of int: heights = [2, 3, 1, 4];

% Decision variables for the positions of the items
array[1..num_items] of var 0..max(widths) * num_items: pos_x;
array[1..num_items] of var 0..max(heights) * num_items: pos_y;

% Decision variables for the dimensions of the larger rectangle
var 0..max(widths) * num_items: total_x;
var 0..max(heights) * num_items: total_y;

% Constraints to ensure no overlap between items
constraint
    forall(i, j in 1..num_items where i < j) (
        pos_x[i] + widths[i] <= pos_x[j] \/ pos_x[j] + widths[j] <= pos_x[i] \/
        pos_y[i] + heights[i] <= pos_y[j] \/ pos_y[j] + heights[j] <= pos_y[i]
    );

% Constraints to ensure all items are within the larger rectangle
constraint
    forall(i in 1..num_items) (
        pos_x[i] + widths[i] <= total_x /\ pos_y[i] + heights[i] <= total_y
    );

% Objective: minimize the area of the larger rectangle
solve minimize total_x * total_y;

% Output the positions of the items and the dimensions of the larger rectangle
