To solve the Car Sequencing problem using Minizinc, we need to define the constraints based on the provided input data. The goal is to create a sequence of car types that respects the constraints on the number of times a property can be present in a group of consecutive timeslots and the demand for each type of car.

Here is the Minizinc model for the problem:

```minizinc
% Input data
int: num_car_types = 6;
int: num_slots = sum(demand);
int: num_properties = 5;

array[1..num_car_types] of int: demand;
array[1..num_properties] of int: at_most;
array[1..num_properties] of int: per_slots;
array[1..num_car_types, 1..num_properties] of int: requires;

% Decision variable: sequence of car types
array[1..num_slots] of var 0..num_car_types-1: sequence;

% Constraint: Respect the demand for each car type
constraint
    forall(t in 0..num_car_types-1) (
        sum(i in 1..num_slots)(bool2int(sequence[i] = t)) = demand[t+1]
    );

% Constraint: Respect the at_most constraint for each property in each group of per_slots
constraint
    forall(p in 1..num_properties) (
        forall(s in 1..num_slots - per_slots[p] + 1) (
            sum(i in s..s + per_slots[p] - 1)(requires[sequence[i]+1, p]) <= at_most[p]
        )
    );

% Output the sequence of car types
output [
    "sequence: [",
    join(",", [show(sequence[i]) | i in 1..num_slots]),
    "]"
];
```

