To solve the All-Interval Series problem for \( n = 12 \) using MiniZinc, we need to define a permutation of the set \(\{0, 1, \ldots, 11\}\) such that the absolute differences between consecutive elements cover all integers from 1 to 11 exactly once.

Here is the MiniZinc model to find such a series:

```minizinc
% Define the number of pitch-classes
int: n = 12;

% Define the array of pitch-classes
array[1..n] of var 0..n-1: x;

% Define the array of differences
array[1..n-1] of var 1..n-1: diffs;

% Constraint: x must be a permutation of 0..n-1
constraint all_different(x);
constraint forall(i in 1..n)(x[i] >= 0 /\ x[i] <= n-1);

% Constraint: diffs must be a permutation of 1..n-1
constraint all_different(diffs);
constraint forall(i in 1..n-1)(diffs[i] >= 1 /\ diffs[i] <= n-1);

% Constraint: diffs[i] = |x[i+1] - x[i]|
constraint forall(i in 1..n-1)(diffs[i] = abs(x[i+1] - x[i]));

% Solve the model
solve satisfy;

% Output the sequence of pitch-classes and the corresponding intervals
output [
    "{",
    join(",", [
        "\"" ++ "x" ++ "\": [" ++ join(",", [show(x[i]) | i in 1..n]) ++ "]",
        "\"" ++ "diffs" ++ "\": [" ++ join(",", [show(diffs[i]) | i in 1..n-1]) ++ "]"
    ]),
    "}"
];
```

